# Deletes the acestream-suite channels that are not online, once the verification has finished.
#
# Installed by hand on the host:
#   install -m 0755 scripts/acestream-suite-prune.py /usr/local/bin/acestream-suite-prune
#   install -m 0644 scripts/acestream-suite-prune.cron /etc/cron.d/acestream-suite-prune
#   /usr/local/bin/acestream-suite-prune --dry-run     # what it would do right now, touching nothing
#
# This is a ONE-SHOT job. Every run asks the database two things: how many channels have never been
# checked, and how many are checked and not alive. While there is anything unchecked it writes one
# line to /var/log/acestream-suite-prune.log and leaves. When the verification has finished it takes the
# backup (/backup/acestream-suite/<stamp>/: the three playlists, the full export, a copy of the database and
# the list of what it is about to delete), deletes through acestream-suite's own API — which takes the check
# history and the screenshots with each channel — regenerates the playlists and writes a guard in
# /var/lib/acestream-suite-prune/done. From then on every run is a no-op, so this cron entry can stay.
#
# Every 20 minutes, off the hour and the half hour: the check is a single count against SQLite, and
# the job needs to notice "the verification just finished" promptly. It takes its own flock, so a
# run that overlaps the previous one says so and exits.
#
# --force exists for the case where the queue was lost (a container restart drops it): it deletes
# what is already known to be dead without waiting, still never touching an unchecked channel.
SHELL=/bin/bash
PATH=/sbin:/bin:/usr/sbin:/usr/bin:/usr/local/bin
10,30,50 * * * * root [ -r /etc/acestream-suite.env ] && . /etc/acestream-suite.env; /usr/local/bin/acestream-suite-prune >/dev/null 2>&1
